Good call — already implemented this. The current code uses `for...of` and short-circuits on `jsxDepth === 0`:

```ts
let best = matches[0];
for (const cur of matches) {
  if (cur.jsxDepth === 0) {
    return cur; // can't get shallower, pick immediately
  }
  if ((cur.jsxDepth ?? Infinity) < (best.jsxDepth ?? Infinity)) {
    best = cur;
  }
}
```

Added a more detailed comment in ce712ba explaining the three cases:

```ts
// Split compound component names (e.g. "Accordion.Root") on the first dot to separate
// the namespace/import binding ("Accordion") from the accessed member ("Root").
//
// Three cases for compound names (dot !== -1):
//   1. No import found for the namespace  → keep componentName + member, no import metadata
//   2. Namespace import (`import * as Ns`) → importName = member (the actual export name)
//   3. Named import (`import { Accordion }`) → importName = original export, member carried separately
//
// Simple names (dot === -1) just look up the import binding directly.
```

`memberAccess` is used on line 429-430 — it accesses the sub-component property from compound components like `Accordion.Root`:
```ts
if (memberAccess) {
  const prop = componentType.getProperty(memberAccess);
```

This is basically straight from the Vitest `memfs` docs. They explicitly show `__mocks__/fs.cjs` and `__mocks__/fs/promises.cjs`, and mention that ESM works too, but then you have to explicitly re-export everything you need.

So instead of this:

```js
const { fs } = require("memfs");
module.exports = fs.promises;
```

you end up with something more like:

```js
import { fs } from "memfs";

export const readFile = fs.promises.readFile.bind(fs.promises);
export const writeFile = fs.promises.writeFile.bind(fs.promises);
export const mkdir = fs.promises.mkdir.bind(fs.promises);
export const readdir = fs.promises.readdir.bind(fs.promises);
export default fs.promises;
```

That's doable, just more annoying and easier to miss exports as tests evolve. So I kept the CJS version here mostly because it's the simplest way to mirror the full `memfs` API shape.

Vitest docs: https://vitest.dev/guide/mocking/file-system

This is mainly meant to handle common "props-derived destructuring" cases, for example:

```ts
const { color = 'info' } = props;
const { color = 'info' } = props ?? {};
const { color = 'info' } = props ?? fallback;
const { color = 'info' } = resolveProps(props);
```

The goal here isn’t to make arbitrary helper logic define the docs. The default still comes from the destructuring pattern written in the component itself, not from inspecting the helper implementation.

I intentionally made the check a bit broader around "expression depends on props" rather than trying to hardcode a few specific syntactic patterns. In practice that’s simpler and more robust than maintaining a narrow allowlist of forms we think are valid.

I also think it’s relatively unlikely to break in a harmful way, because the initializer still has to be props-derived for us to consider it at all. So this is meant as a pragmatic heuristic for common real-world component code, not as a perfect semantic proof.
